Where it came from
------------------

The faces were copied out of an OS X 10.9 (Mavericks) installation image's
System/Library/Fonts/Base.  Their names, their contents and their bytes are
Apple's, unchanged; nothing here was redrawn, traced or renamed.

The wallpapers of that system are not here.  They were taken out of the same
image once and removed again: they are thirty times the size of the faces, and
a desktop picks its pictures from a wallpaper collection rather than from its
dotfiles.

Why it is here
--------------

Heks is a desktop modelled on Mavericks - the menu bar, the typefaces, the
wallpapers - and a desktop modelled on another system needs that system's
materials to look like it.  The faces are the part no GNU/Linux distribution
ships, and they are what the menu bar's metrics were measured against.  They are
kept in this checkout so that installing them needs no second download and no
installation image on the machine.

What you may do with it
-----------------------

Nothing in heks-linux's MIT license grants any right to these files: the MIT
license covers the Scheme modules, the scripts and the documentation, not the
artwork beside them.  Apple's own terms govern what may be done with the faces,
and they are not reproduced here.  If you are redistributing heks-linux, or
building something on top of it, decide for yourself whether carrying these
files is something you may do - the safe answer is to drop this directory and
point `mavericks-assets-dir` at your own.

If you hold the rights to this material and want it removed, open an issue on
the project and it goes.
